    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm studying for sec+ and i'm kinda stuck on one thing. if the password is already being hashed, why do we even need to salt it? wouldnt the hash already make it secure anyway?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A hash function makes data unique only if the input is also unique; without a salt, two users with identical passwords generate the exact same hash, making it easy to crack using precomputed rainbow tables. Salt adds random data before hashing, ensuring that even identical passwords produce different hashes and rendering rainbow tables ineffective. Essentially, the salt forces attackers to hash every potential password for each user individually rather than checking a single dictionary against all users at once.",
      "source": "target",
