    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so like, if an attacker has a list of common passwords and their hashes already جاهز (ready), they can just compare them? how exactly does adding that random salt bit stop them from just adding the salt to their list too?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The attacker cannot simply add the salt to their precomputed hash list because different salts change the mathematical input to the hash function in a non-linear way, meaning \"Password + Salt A\" will never match the hash of \"Password + Salt B\". Since salts are unique to each user and are not stored with the hash list, the attacker must perform a new, individual brute-force calculation for every single password guess against each user's specific salt, which is computationally infeasible at scale.",
