    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "ok i think i get it. so the salt basically makes every user's hash unique even if they use the same password. is there any way an attacker could still bypass it, or is it basically foolproof if the salt is long enough and truly random?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While a strong random salt makes rainbow tables useless, attackers can still bypass defenses by using brute-force attacks if the password is weak or by stealing the database directly to perform pure dictionary attacks. However, modern systems mitigate this by combining unique salts with unique \"hashes of salts\" (called random salts) stored alongside the password hashes to prevent online attacks or offline dictionary attacks. With sufficiently long, random salts and slow hashing algorithms like bcrypt, bcrypt is considered highly secure and effectively foolproof against automated cracking unless millions of hashes are compromised.",
